      <section class="book-spread" id="spread-evidence" data-spread="evidence" aria-labelledby="title-evidence">
        <div class="book-page lesson-page">
          <p class="book-kicker">01 / HARDWARE FOUNDATIONS · MEASUREMENT</p>
          <h3 id="title-evidence">Fast numbers are exciting.<br/><em>Trustworthy numbers are useful.</em></h3>
          <p>GPU work is often asynchronous: the CPU can queue a calculation before the GPU has finished it. That is why we measure elapsed device time using <strong>CUDA events</strong> and proper synchronization.</p>
          <p>We also <strong>warm up</strong> the GPU, repeat experiments, and inspect the variation. One unusually fast or slow number does not explain a system.</p>
          <div class="analogy"><span>OUR RULE</span><p>Keep measured results separate from estimates. An estimate can help explain a concept, but it cannot prove an optimization worked.</p></div>
          <p>We now have the vocabulary needed to follow a real model through memory, matrix calculations, and token generation.</p>
          <span class="page-number">12</span>
        </div>
        <div class="book-page experiment">
          <div class="experiment-heading"><span>TRY IT / PERFORMANCE DETECTIVE</span><span class="live-tag">BENCHMARK EVIDENCE</span></div>
          <p>One batch-32 run reported a median of <strong>2.7542 ms</strong> but a fastest trial of <strong>0.1564 ms</strong>. Which number proves steady-state GPU performance?</p>
          <div class="hardware-answer-options"><button data-evidence-answer="median">The median</button><button data-evidence-answer="fastest">The fastest</button><button data-evidence-answer="neither">Neither alone</button></div>
          <p class="demo-explanation" data-evidence-feedback aria-live="polite">Choose an answer, then compare what each measurement can and cannot establish.</p>
          <div class="hardware-metrics"><div><span>Memory capacity</span><strong>6 GiB</strong></div><div><span>Copy bandwidth</span><strong>237.5 GB/s</strong></div><div><span>Best GEMM</span><strong>43.73 TFLOP/s</strong></div><div><span>SM count</span><strong>32</strong></div></div>
          <p class="book-note">These are notebook observations for the RTX A3000, not theoretical GPU peak specifications.</p>
          <span class="page-number">13</span>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="book-spread q2-spread" id="spread-qwen" data-spread="qwen" aria-labelledby="title-qwen">
        <div class="book-page lesson-page">
          <p class="book-kicker">CHAPTER 02 / LESSON 01</p>
          <h3 id="title-qwen">Text<br/><em>becomes tokens</em></h3>
          <p>A prompt becomes token IDs before the model can do any arithmetic. Our Qwen2.5-1.5B-Instruct has 1.54 billion parameters; its FP16 weights occupy approximately 2.88 GiB.</p>
          <div class="analogy"><span>THE MENTAL MODEL</span><p>Tokenize a sentence and watch the GPU memory fill.</p></div>
          <p class="q2-evidence">02.1 · 1,543,714,304 parameters</p>
          <span class="page-number">20</span>
        </div>
        <div class="book-page experiment q2-experiment" data-q2-demo="qwen"><div class="experiment-heading"><span>TRY IT / INTERACTIVE MODEL</span><span class="live-tag">CHAPTER 02</span></div><div class="q2-hero"><strong>THE TOKEN PIPELINE</strong><div class="q2-tokens" data-q2-tokens></div><div class="q2-flow">TEXT → TOKEN IDs → EMBEDDINGS → MODEL → LOGITS</div></div><label class="range-label" for="q2-token-input">Example prompt</label><select id="q2-token-input" class="q2-select"><option value="0">The GPU reads tokens</option><option value="1">One token at a time</option><option value="2">Cache keys and values</option></select><div class="q2-stat"><span>FP16 model weights</span><strong>2.88 GiB</strong></div><p class="demo-explanation" data-q2-explain>These word-like blocks are illustrative, not the model tokenizer's exact token IDs.</p><details class="q2-reasoning" open>
            <summary>THE ENGINEERING REASONING <span>Question → Evidence → Why</span></summary>
            <div class="q2-reason-body">
              <h4>01 · The question</h4><p>What actually occupies GPU memory before the first token?</p>
              <h4>02 · Our hypothesis</h4><p>FP16 stores each parameter in two bytes, so the weights alone should need roughly 3.09 GB (2.88 GiB).</p>
              <h4>03 · How we tested it</h4><p>Experiment 02.1 loaded Qwen2.5-1.5B-Instruct on an RTX A3000 Laptop GPU, counted parameters and recorded allocated CUDA memory, then tokenized and generated a short answer.</p>
              <h4>04 · What the numbers say</h4><p>1,543,714,304 parameters × 2 bytes = 3,087,428,608 weight bytes. Allocated memory after loading was 3,088,347,136 bytes. The 33-token prompt took 47.82 ms median warm prefill; 26 generated tokens took 1,247.54 ms in that run.</p>
              <h4>05 · Why it makes sense</h4><p>The near agreement between theoretical weight storage and measured allocation tells us the model weights dominate the initial GPU footprint. But memory needed during inference also includes temporary activations, KV cache and framework allocations. Cold prefill (371.07 ms) and warm prefill (47.82 ms) differed substantially; this is why warmups matter.</p>
              <h4>06 · What we cannot conclude</h4><p>Parameter count predicts weight storage, not total peak memory or end-to-end latency. The sample run cannot establish a universal throughput for this GPU.</p>
            </div>
          </details>
          <span class="page-number">21</span></div>
      </section>
      <section class="book-spread q2-spread" id="spread-prefill" data-spread="prefill" aria-labelledby="title-prefill">
        <div class="book-page lesson-page">
          <p class="book-kicker">CHAPTER 02 / LESSON 02</p>
          <h3 id="title-prefill">Prefill<br/><em>reads together</em></h3>
          <p>Prefill processes the entire prompt in one forward pass. The model builds the initial attention state and produces logits for the first new token. Its large matrices can reuse weight data.</p>
          <div class="analogy"><span>THE MENTAL MODEL</span><p>Choose the prompt length and watch a whole row of tokens pass through the model.</p></div>
          <p class="q2-evidence">02.5 · 128-token prefill, batch 1: 151.12 ms</p>
          <span class="page-number">22</span>
        </div>
        <div class="book-page experiment q2-experiment" data-q2-demo="prefill"><div class="experiment-heading"><span>TRY IT / INTERACTIVE MODEL</span><span class="live-tag">CHAPTER 02</span></div><div class="q2-stage"><strong>PARALLEL PROMPT PASS</strong><div class="q2-token-grid" data-q2-prefill-grid></div><div class="q2-flow">ALL PROMPT POSITIONS → TRANSFORMER → FIRST-TOKEN LOGITS</div></div><label class="range-label" for="q2-prefill-size">Prompt positions <output id="q2-prefill-value">32</output></label><input id="q2-prefill-size" type="range" min="1" max="4" value="2"/><button class="book-button" data-q2-prefill-play>Run prefill →</button><p class="demo-explanation">Each block represents several token positions. Larger prompts mean more work; the 151.12 ms measurement applies only to the 128-token benchmark.</p><details class="q2-reasoning" open>
            <summary>THE ENGINEERING REASONING <span>Question → Evidence → Why</span></summary>
            <div class="q2-reason-body">
              <h4>01 · The question</h4><p>Why can the GPU process a whole prompt efficiently?</p>
              <h4>02 · Our hypothesis</h4><p>Processing many token positions together creates matrix–matrix operations with more opportunities to reuse model weights than single-token decoding.</p>
              <h4>03 · How we tested it</h4><p>Experiment 02.5 measured one forward pass at a fixed 128-token context, FP16 SDPA, batch 1 and 4, with 3 warmups and 8 trials. Wall time and CUDA-event time were collected separately.</p>
              <h4>04 · What the numbers say</h4><p>Batch 1 prefill median: 151.12 ms wall, 150.87 ms CUDA event. Batch 4 prefill median: 334.79 ms wall, 334.60 ms CUDA event. These are separate workloads; batch 4 processes 512 prompt tokens in total.</p>
              <h4>05 · Why it makes sense</h4><p>Prefill has many token rows available simultaneously. Larger GEMM tiles can amortize weight access across rows, improving useful arithmetic per byte of weights. Batch 4 requires more work and took longer overall; dividing 334.79 ms by four is a throughput-oriented normalization, not an individual request's latency.</p>
              <h4>06 · What we cannot conclude</h4><p>A 128-token prefill measurement does not by itself prove compute saturation. CUDA-event duration is not a hardware-counter measurement of SM occupancy or memory traffic.</p>
            </div>
          </details>
          <span class="page-number">23</span></div>
      </section>
      <section class="book-spread q2-spread" id="spread-decode" data-spread="decode" aria-labelledby="title-decode">
        <div class="book-page lesson-page">
          <p class="book-kicker">CHAPTER 02 / LESSON 03</p>
          <h3 id="title-decode">Decode<br/><em>follows a rhythm</em></h3>
          <p>Generation is autoregressive: select the next token, append it to the sequence, and repeat. Time to first token and per-token decode latency measure different parts of the journey.</p>
          <div class="analogy"><span>THE MENTAL MODEL</span><p>Step through a four-token response, one prediction at a time.</p></div>
          <p class="q2-evidence">02.5 · cached decode step, batch 1: 117.26 ms</p>
          <span class="page-number">24</span>
        </div>
        <div class="book-page experiment q2-experiment" data-q2-demo="decode"><div class="experiment-heading"><span>TRY IT / INTERACTIVE MODEL</span><span class="live-tag">CHAPTER 02</span></div><div class="q2-stage"><strong>AUTOREGRESSIVE GENERATION</strong><div class="q2-token-grid q2-decode-grid" data-q2-decode-grid><span class="q2-cell is-on">Prompt</span></div><div class="q2-flow">PREDICT → APPEND → REPEAT</div></div><div class="demo-controls"><button class="book-button" data-q2-decode-next>Generate next token →</button><button class="quiet-button" data-q2-decode-reset>Reset</button></div><div class="q2-stat"><span>Generated tokens</span><strong data-q2-decode-count>0 / 4</strong></div><p class="demo-explanation">The four words are an illustration of greedy decoding, not a live model inference call.</p><details class="q2-reasoning" open>
            <summary>THE ENGINEERING REASONING <span>Question → Evidence → Why</span></summary>
            <div class="q2-reason-body">
              <h4>01 · The question</h4><p>Why does generating 26 tokens take much longer than one prefill?</p>
              <h4>02 · Our hypothesis</h4><p>Each next token depends on the previous prediction, so decode forwards execute sequentially even though work inside each forward is parallel.</p>
              <h4>03 · How we tested it</h4><p>Experiments 02.1–02.3 compared model.generate with manual cached decoding and measured paired prefill and first-token timing. Experiment 02.5 separately measured a single cached decode forward using a fresh prebuilt KV cache outside the timed region.</p>
              <h4>04 · What the numbers say</h4><p>Experiment 02.3 paired prefill median: 44.28 ms; paired TTFT proxy: 44.47 ms. Its 26-token generate run took 1,216.65 ms (21.37 output tokens/s including prefill). Experiment 02.5 measured a 117.26 ms median single decode forward for a different 128-token workload.</p>
              <h4>05 · Why it makes sense</h4><p>TTFT includes the prompt-processing path and first-token selection; it must not be compared with an unrelated prefill median. Output throughput computed from full generate time includes prefill. Single-step decode forward excludes generation-loop logic and token selection, so it is not the same metric as end-to-end tokens/s.</p>
              <h4>06 · What we cannot conclude</h4><p>Experiment 02.3 and 02.5 used different prompt lengths and conditions; their timing baselines differ substantially. The cause has not been isolated. Manual generation and generate also produced nonmatching token-ID sequences in 02.3.</p>
            </div>
          </details>
          <span class="page-number">25</span></div>
      </section>
      <section class="book-spread q2-spread" id="spread-qcache" data-spread="qcache" aria-labelledby="title-qcache">
        <div class="book-page lesson-page">
          <p class="book-kicker">CHAPTER 02 / LESSON 04</p>
          <h3 id="title-qcache">Keep<br/><em>keys and values</em></h3>
          <p>Without a KV cache, generation recomputes attention projections for earlier positions. With a cache, previous keys and values can be reused. Caching has a memory cost but saves repeated work.</p>
          <div class="analogy"><span>THE MENTAL MODEL</span><p>Switch cache ON or OFF and choose a measured prompt length.</p></div>
          <p class="q2-evidence">02.4 · 512-token prompt: 3.05× cache speedup</p>
          <span class="page-number">26</span>
        </div>
        <div class="book-page experiment q2-experiment" data-q2-demo="qcache"><div class="experiment-heading"><span>TRY IT / INTERACTIVE MODEL</span><span class="live-tag">CHAPTER 02</span></div><div class="q2-stage"><strong>MEASURED GENERATION / 32 OUTPUT TOKENS</strong><div class="q2-compare"><div><span>CACHE ON</span><i data-q2-cache-on></i><b data-q2-cache-on-time>4.07 s</b></div><div><span>CACHE OFF</span><i data-q2-cache-off></i><b data-q2-cache-off-time>12.39 s</b></div></div></div><label class="range-label" for="q2-cache-context">Prompt tokens <output id="q2-cache-context-value">512</output></label><input id="q2-cache-context" type="range" min="0" max="2" value="2"/><div class="q2-stat"><span>Cache speedup</span><strong data-q2-cache-speed>3.05×</strong></div><p class="demo-explanation">Measured end-to-end generation times from Experiment 02.4, batch 1. Output IDs matched with cache on and off.</p><details class="q2-reasoning" open>
            <summary>THE ENGINEERING REASONING <span>Question → Evidence → Why</span></summary>
            <div class="q2-reason-body">
              <h4>01 · The question</h4><p>Why does caching help more when the prompt gets longer?</p>
              <h4>02 · Our hypothesis</h4><p>Reusing prior keys and values avoids recomputing the entire prefix at every new-token step; savings should grow with context length.</p>
              <h4>03 · How we tested it</h4><p>Experiment 02.4 generated 32 output tokens at batch 1 with cache enabled and disabled. Context lengths were 32, 128 and 512 tokens; results are median end-to-end times over repeated trials. Generated token IDs were checked for equality.</p>
              <h4>04 · What the numbers say</h4><p>At 32 tokens: 3.806 s ON versus 4.422 s OFF (1.16×). At 128: 3.849 s versus 5.847 s (1.52×). At 512: 4.070 s versus 12.395 s (3.05×). Token IDs matched for cache ON and OFF.</p>
              <h4>05 · Why it makes sense</h4><p>With caching, each decode step still attends to previous positions, but does not recompute their K/V projections. Without caching, each successive step repeats increasing amounts of prior-sequence work. Thus longer prefixes magnify the penalty. The cache occupies memory: Experiment 02.3 measured 28,672 bytes of KV growth per additional token.</p>
              <h4>06 · What we cannot conclude</h4><p>These speedups apply to this model, context range and 32-token generation workload. Cache ON can add some prefill bookkeeping, and cache memory grows with sequence length.</p>
            </div>
          </details>
          <span class="page-number">27</span></div>
      </section>
      <section class="book-spread q2-spread" id="spread-qbatch" data-spread="qbatch" aria-labelledby="title-qbatch">
        <div class="book-page lesson-page">
          <p class="book-kicker">CHAPTER 02 / LESSON 05</p>
          <h3 id="title-qbatch">Batch<br/><em>requests together</em></h3>
          <p>A single request makes a thin matrix multiply. Multiple requests can use the same weight matrix in one larger operation. Aggregate throughput rises even when individual batch latency rises slightly.</p>
          <div class="analogy"><span>THE MENTAL MODEL</span><p>Switch between batches of 1, 2 and 4 and compare measured throughput.</p></div>
          <p class="q2-evidence">02.4 · batch 1: 8.27 tok/s; batch 4: 30.79 tok/s</p>
          <span class="page-number">28</span>
        </div>
        <div class="book-page experiment q2-experiment" data-q2-demo="qbatch"><div class="experiment-heading"><span>TRY IT / INTERACTIVE MODEL</span><span class="live-tag">CHAPTER 02</span></div><div class="q2-stage"><strong>REQUESTS SHARING THE GPU</strong><div class="q2-batch-rows" data-q2-batch-rows></div></div><label class="range-label" for="q2-batch-size">Batch size <output id="q2-batch-value">1</output></label><input id="q2-batch-size" type="range" min="0" max="2" value="0"/><div class="q2-stat"><span>Aggregate throughput</span><strong data-q2-batch-throughput>8.27 tok/s</strong></div><div class="q2-stat"><span>Total batch latency</span><strong data-q2-batch-latency>3.87 s</strong></div><p class="demo-explanation">Measured from Experiment 02.4: 128-token prompt, 32 output tokens per request, cache on. More throughput does not mean lower latency for each request.</p><details class="q2-reasoning" open>
            <summary>THE ENGINEERING REASONING <span>Question → Evidence → Why</span></summary>
            <div class="q2-reason-body">
              <h4>01 · The question</h4><p>How can four requests finish in almost the same time as one?</p>
              <h4>02 · Our hypothesis</h4><p>Combining requests increases the matrix's token-row dimension and reuses weights across requests, raising aggregate throughput.</p>
              <h4>03 · How we tested it</h4><p>Experiment 02.4 held prompt length at 128 tokens and output length at 32 per request, with KV cache enabled. It compared batches of 1, 2 and 4, recording full batch wall time and aggregate generated tokens per second.</p>
              <h4>04 · What the numbers say</h4><p>Batch 1: 3,868.54 ms, 8.27 tokens/s. Batch 2: 3,986.17 ms, 16.06 tokens/s. Batch 4: 4,157.74 ms, 30.79 tokens/s. Batch 4's aggregate throughput was about 3.72× batch 1, while batch latency rose about 7.5%.</p>
              <h4>05 · Why it makes sense</h4><p>Batching improves aggregate throughput because the GPU can process more request rows while reusing weight data. It does not make each request complete four times faster: the whole batch's latency is experienced by each member. This is the difference between throughput and latency, essential in serving systems.</p>
              <h4>06 · What we cannot conclude</h4><p>This is fixed-shape batching, not a benchmark of continuous batching, queueing or real serving traffic. We have not isolated whether the improvement is solely from weight reuse.</p>
            </div>
          </details>
          <span class="page-number">29</span></div>
      </section>
      <section class="book-spread q2-spread" id="spread-gemm" data-spread="gemm" aria-labelledby="title-gemm">
        <div class="book-page lesson-page">
          <p class="book-kicker">CHAPTER 02 / LESSON 06</p>
          <h3 id="title-gemm">The<br/><em>shape changes everything</em></h3>
          <p>Prefill is matrix–matrix-like: many token rows multiplied by weights. Batch-one decode is matrix–vector-like: one token row multiplied by the same weights. More arithmetic does not imply proportionally more time.</p>
          <div class="analogy"><span>THE MENTAL MODEL</span><p>Switch between 128 rows and one row; observe the same weight block being reused.</p></div>
          <p class="q2-evidence">02.5 · profiled matmul: prefill 117.24 ms; decode ~97.71 ms per step</p>
          <span class="page-number">30</span>
        </div>
        <div class="book-page experiment q2-experiment" data-q2-demo="gemm"><div class="experiment-heading"><span>TRY IT / INTERACTIVE MODEL</span><span class="live-tag">CHAPTER 02</span></div><div class="q2-stage"><strong>THE WEIGHTS DO NOT SHRINK</strong><div class="q2-matrix-demo"><div class="q2-input-matrix" data-q2-matrix-input></div><span>×</span><div class="q2-weight-matrix"></div><span>=</span><div class="q2-output-matrix" data-q2-matrix-output></div></div><div class="q2-flow" data-q2-matrix-label>PREFILL · MATRIX × MATRIX</div></div><div class="demo-controls"><button class="book-button" data-q2-matrix-toggle>Switch to decode →</button></div><p class="demo-explanation">Matrix rows are schematic. The real profiler's 293.13 ms decode matmul total covers three steps; ~97.71 ms is its per-step normalized total.</p><details class="q2-reasoning" open>
            <summary>THE ENGINEERING REASONING <span>Question → Evidence → Why</span></summary>
            <div class="q2-reason-body">
              <h4>01 · The question</h4><p>How can prefill process 128 tokens for only slightly more matrix-multiply time than one decode step?</p>
              <h4>02 · Our hypothesis</h4><p>Prefill uses efficient larger GEMMs; batch-one decode uses thin GEMM/GEMV-like workloads with much less reuse of each weight.</p>
              <h4>03 · How we tested it</h4><p>Experiment 02.5 profiled one prefill forward and three consecutive cached decode forwards. We compared PyTorch aten::mm cumulative CUDA times and checked the number of calls before interpreting totals.</p>
              <h4>04 · What the numbers say</h4><p>Prefill aten::mm: 117.24 ms over 113 calls. Decode aten::mm: 293.13 ms over 339 calls across three steps, or about 97.71 ms per step. The raw 293.13-versus-117.24 comparison is misleading because decode contains three passes.</p>
              <h4>05 · Why it makes sense</h4><p>Prefill performs roughly 128 times the dense linear-layer arithmetic of a one-token step at the same batch, but can reuse weight tiles across 128 rows and use larger Ampere FP16 GEMM kernels. Decode includes small CUTLASS WMMA GEMMs and GEMV kernels. The key is utilization and reuse, not FLOP count alone.</p>
              <h4>06 · What we cannot conclude</h4><p>Dividing the three-step cumulative profiler total by three is an approximation, not a separate benchmark. Profiler overhead alters timing, and operator rows must not be added to the underlying kernel rows.</p>
            </div>
          </details>
          <span class="page-number">31</span></div>
      </section>
      <section class="book-spread q2-spread" id="spread-intensity" data-spread="intensity" aria-labelledby="title-intensity">
        <div class="book-page lesson-page">
          <p class="book-kicker">CHAPTER 02 / LESSON 07</p>
          <h3 id="title-intensity">FLOPs<br/><em>per byte</em></h3>
          <p>Arithmetic intensity compares arithmetic with data movement. For a simplified FP16 weight-only linear-layer model, batch-one decode is about 1 FLOP/byte and batch-four about 4. These are analytical estimates, not DRAM measurements.</p>
          <div class="analogy"><span>THE MENTAL MODEL</span><p>Move the batch slider and watch the arithmetic-intensity marker move.</p></div>
          <p class="q2-evidence">02.4 · estimated weight-only AI: 1, 2, 4 FLOPs/byte</p>
          <span class="page-number">32</span>
        </div>
        <div class="book-page experiment q2-experiment" data-q2-demo="intensity"><div class="experiment-heading"><span>TRY IT / INTERACTIVE MODEL</span><span class="live-tag">CHAPTER 02</span></div><div class="q2-stage"><strong>WEIGHT-ONLY ARITHMETIC INTENSITY</strong><div class="q2-ai-axis"><div class="q2-ai-marker" data-q2-ai-marker>●</div><div class="q2-ai-ticks"><span>0</span><span>1</span><span>2</span><span>3</span><span>4</span></div></div><div class="q2-flow">MORE WEIGHT REUSE → MORE FLOPs / WEIGHT BYTE</div></div><label class="range-label" for="q2-ai-batch">Batch size <output id="q2-ai-batch-value">1</output></label><input id="q2-ai-batch" type="range" min="1" max="4" value="1"/><div class="q2-stat"><span>Approximate intensity</span><strong data-q2-ai>1 FLOP/byte</strong></div><p class="demo-explanation">Analytical dense-layer approximation only: excludes other memory traffic, attention, and caching. This is not measured DRAM bandwidth or a complete roofline measurement.</p><details class="q2-reasoning" open>
            <summary>THE ENGINEERING REASONING <span>Question → Evidence → Why</span></summary>
            <div class="q2-reason-body">
              <h4>01 · The question</h4><p>Can we predict when matrix multiplication will be constrained by bytes rather than FLOPs?</p>
              <h4>02 · Our hypothesis</h4><p>For a simplified dense FP16 layer, each additional simultaneous token row increases arithmetic performed per byte of weight storage.</p>
              <h4>03 · How we tested it</h4><p>Estimate dense decode FLOPs as 2 × parameter count × batch size, and weight-only bytes as 2 × parameter count. Divide to obtain an idealized arithmetic-intensity proxy. Compare this with the batch timing measurements.</p>
              <h4>04 · What the numbers say</h4><p>Weight-only intensity is approximately 1, 2 and 4 FLOPs/byte for batch sizes 1, 2 and 4. Experiment 02.5 estimated 0.0264 TFLOP/s for batch 1 and 0.1020 TFLOP/s for batch 4, based on dense FLOPs divided by measured decode time.</p>
              <h4>05 · Why it makes sense</h4><p>More rows reuse the same model weights, so arithmetic increases faster than the minimum weight bytes. This shifts the idealized workload rightward on a roofline plot. But the simple estimate excludes KV traffic, activations, cache hits and kernel inefficiency, so it is an explanatory model rather than a measured roofline point.</p>
              <h4>06 · What we cannot conclude</h4><p>The 26 GB/s weight-bytes/time value is NOT physical DRAM bandwidth. Without hardware counters, we cannot claim DRAM saturation or establish an achieved bandwidth ceiling.</p>
            </div>
          </details>
          <span class="page-number">33</span></div>
      </section>
      <section class="book-spread q2-spread" id="spread-profile" data-spread="profile" aria-labelledby="title-profile">
        <div class="book-page lesson-page">
          <p class="book-kicker">CHAPTER 02 / LESSON 08</p>
          <h3 id="title-profile">A<br/><em>profiler is a microscope</em></h3>
          <p>A profiler identifies operator and kernel activity. Compare cumulative totals only for matching numbers of calls: our decode capture contained three steps, while prefill contained one.</p>
          <div class="analogy"><span>THE MENTAL MODEL</span><p>Toggle prefill/decode and inspect selected profiled kernel categories.</p></div>
          <p class="q2-evidence">02.5 · aten::mm: 113 calls prefill, 339 calls across 3 decode steps</p>
          <span class="page-number">34</span>
        </div>
        <div class="book-page experiment q2-experiment" data-q2-demo="profile"><div class="experiment-heading"><span>TRY IT / INTERACTIVE MODEL</span><span class="live-tag">CHAPTER 02</span></div><div class="q2-stage"><strong>SELECTED PROFILED KERNEL TIME</strong><div class="q2-compare q2-profile-bars"><div><span>aten::mm</span><i data-q2-profile-mm></i><b data-q2-profile-mm-value>117.24 ms</b></div><div><span>ATTENTION</span><i data-q2-profile-attn></i><b data-q2-profile-attn-value>—</b></div></div></div><label class="range-label" for="q2-profile-mode">Capture</label><select id="q2-profile-mode" class="q2-select"><option value="prefill">Prefill · one forward pass</option><option value="decode">Decode · three forward passes</option></select><div class="q2-stat"><span>aten::mm calls</span><strong data-q2-profile-calls>113</strong></div><p class="demo-explanation" data-q2-profile-note>PyTorch profiler cumulative operator time. Do not add kernel-level rows to operator-level rows; they can describe the same work.</p><details class="q2-reasoning" open>
            <summary>THE ENGINEERING REASONING <span>Question → Evidence → Why</span></summary>
            <div class="q2-reason-body">
              <h4>01 · The question</h4><p>Which GPU operations consume time, and how do we avoid misreading a profiler?</p>
              <h4>02 · Our hypothesis</h4><p>Prefill should favor larger GEMM kernels; decode should favor smaller GEMM/GEMV-like kernels. Attention need not dominate short-context decode.</p>
              <h4>03 · How we tested it</h4><p>Experiment 02.5 used torch.profiler for one prefill forward, three sequential cached decode forwards, and a short generate call. It recorded top operators and kernel names separately from unprofiled wall/CUDA-event timing.</p>
              <h4>04 · What the numbers say</h4><p>Prefill: aten::mm 117.24 ms across 113 calls. Decode: aten::mm 293.13 ms across 339 calls; efficient-attention operator 1.37 ms across 84 calls. Decode includes CUTLASS WMMA 16×16 kernels and GEMV kernels; prefill includes Ampere FP16 GEMMs.</p>
              <h4>05 · Why it makes sense</h4><p>The profiler reveals what runs, but its report is hierarchical: aten::mm and the named CUDA kernels can represent the same work, so adding them double-counts. Decode was profiled for three steps, which explains its larger cumulative matmul total. Short-context attention is small in this capture; that does not imply attention is always negligible.</p>
              <h4>06 · What we cannot conclude</h4><p>Profiler timing is perturbed by profiling overhead. These are not measured DRAM transactions or hardware-counter FLOPs. A 02.3-versus-02.4 performance discrepancy remains unresolved.</p>
            </div>
          </details>
          <span class="page-number">35</span></div>
      </section>
      <section class="book-spread q2-spread" id="spread-case" data-spread="case" aria-labelledby="title-case">
        <div class="book-page lesson-page">
          <p class="book-kicker">CHAPTER 02 / LESSON 09</p>
          <h3 id="title-case">What<br/><em>did Qwen teach us?</em></h3>
          <p>Our five experiments connect model memory, first-token latency, KV reuse, batching, and profiling. We can identify likely bottlenecks without pretending a FLOPs/byte estimate is a physical bandwidth measurement.</p>
          <div class="analogy"><span>THE MENTAL MODEL</span><p>Explore the results dashboard and choose the next optimization hypothesis.</p></div>
          <p class="q2-evidence">02.1–02.5 · local RTX A3000 Laptop GPU</p>
          <span class="page-number">36</span>
        </div>
        <div class="book-page experiment q2-experiment" data-q2-demo="case"><div class="experiment-heading"><span>TRY IT / INTERACTIVE MODEL</span><span class="live-tag">CHAPTER 02</span></div><div class="q2-stage"><strong>FIVE EXPERIMENTS · ONE MODEL</strong><div class="q2-case-grid"><div><span>FP16 WEIGHTS</span><b>2.88 GiB</b></div><div><span>KV CACHE SPEEDUP</span><b>3.05×</b></div><div><span>BATCH-4 THROUGHPUT</span><b>30.79 tok/s</b></div><div><span>DECODE STEP</span><b>117.26 ms</b></div></div></div><label class="range-label" for="q2-case-choice">Explore the conclusion</label><select id="q2-case-choice" class="q2-select"><option value="0">What does caching prove?</option><option value="1">What does batching prove?</option><option value="2">What remains unproven?</option></select><p class="demo-explanation" data-q2-case-explain>KV reuse makes long-context generation faster. In Experiment 02.4, cached and uncached output token IDs matched.</p><p class="book-note">Local Qwen2.5-1.5B-Instruct FP16 results on RTX A3000 Laptop GPU. Results across experiments have different workloads; do not combine them as one benchmark.</p><details class="q2-reasoning" open>
            <summary>THE ENGINEERING REASONING <span>Question → Evidence → Why</span></summary>
            <div class="q2-reason-body">
              <h4>01 · The question</h4><p>What have the five experiments actually established?</p>
              <h4>02 · Our hypothesis</h4><p>A reliable optimization strategy starts with correctness and controlled measurements, then uses profiling to select the next experiment rather than guessing at bottlenecks.</p>
              <h4>03 · How we tested it</h4><p>Experiment 02.1 established memory and generation basics; 02.2 inspected KV growth; 02.3 controlled TTFT and decode; 02.4 tested cache and batching; 02.5 profiled GPU execution and simple roofline proxies.</p>
              <h4>04 · What the numbers say</h4><p>Model weights: 2.88 GiB. KV growth: 28 KiB/token. Cache speedup at context 512: 3.05×. Aggregate batch throughput: 8.27 to 30.79 tokens/s. Profiled decode matmul: 293.13 ms over three steps.</p>
              <h4>05 · Why it makes sense</h4><p>Each result answers a different question: weight bytes explain model footprint; cache growth explains memory scaling; cache ON/OFF isolates repeated computation; batching tests shared weight processing; profiling identifies operator and kernel families. Combining them gives a coherent performance model without conflating timing methods.</p>
              <h4>06 · What we cannot conclude</h4><p>The unexplained 02.3 versus 02.4 throughput difference is an open question. The next controlled test should compare eager cached decode and CUDA Graph replay on exactly the same workload, with correctness checks.</p>
            </div>
          </details>
          <span class="page-number">37</span></div>
      </section>

      <section class="book-spread" id="spread-tokens" data-spread="tokens" aria-labelledby="title-tokens">
        <div class="book-page lesson-page">
          <p class="book-kicker">01 / THE GENERATION LOOP</p>
          <h3 id="title-tokens">A reply is built<br/><em>one token at a time.</em></h3>
          <p>First, the model processes the prompt. That is <strong>prefill</strong>. Then it generates a token, appends it to the context, and runs again. That is <strong>decode</strong>.</p>
          <div class="analogy"><span>THINK OF IT THIS WAY</span><p>Read the question once. Write the answer a small piece at a time, checking what came before.</p></div>
          <p>Prompt tokens can be processed in parallel with a causal mask. Generated tokens depend on earlier choices, so their steps happen in sequence.</p>
          <a class="chapter-link" href={`${base}/transformer-forward-pass/`}>Go deeper: transformer inference mechanics →</a>
          <span class="page-number">02</span>
        </div>
        <div class="book-page experiment" data-token-demo>
          <div class="experiment-heading"><span>TRY IT / FOLLOW THE TOKENS</span><span class="live-tag">STEP BY STEP</span></div>
          <p class="demo-caption">Illustrative tokens, not a tokenizer trace.</p>
          <div class="token-question"><span>THE PROMPT</span><div><b class="prompt-token">The</b><b class="prompt-token">sky</b><b class="prompt-token">is</b></div></div>
          <div class="model-box"><span>TRANSFORMER</span><div class="model-layers"><i></i><i></i><i></i><i></i></div><small data-token-phase>Ready to read the prompt</small></div>
          <div class="token-answer"><span>THE CONTINUATION</span><div>{['blue', 'on', 'a', 'clear', 'day', '.'].map((t, i) => <b class="output-token" data-token={i} aria-hidden="true">{t}</b>)}</div></div>
          <div class="demo-controls"><button class="book-button" data-token-next>Run prefill →</button><button class="quiet-button" data-token-reset>Reset</button></div>
          <p class="demo-explanation" data-token-explanation aria-live="polite">Start with prefill. The three prompt tokens pass through the model together.</p>
          <span class="page-number">03</span>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="book-spread" id="spread-cache" data-spread="cache" aria-labelledby="title-cache">
        <div class="book-page lesson-page">
          <p class="book-kicker">02 / THE KV CACHE</p>
          <h3 id="title-cache">Keep the past.<br/><em>Compute the new.</em></h3>
          <p>Without a cache, a basic implementation recomputes old tokens’ keys and values at every generation step.</p>
          <p>A <strong>KV cache</strong> stores those keys and values. Each new token adds its own entry and attends to the stored context.</p>
          <div class="analogy"><span>THE TRADEOFF</span><p>Less repeated computation. More memory held for every active request.</p></div>
          <p>The cache does <em>not</em> remove attention to earlier tokens. Decode still reads the growing cache, which is why long contexts cost memory and bandwidth.</p>
          <a class="chapter-link" href={`${base}/kv-cache/`}>Go deeper: KV cache optimization →</a>
          <span class="page-number">04</span>
        </div>
        <div class="book-page experiment">
          <div class="experiment-heading"><span>TRY IT / REUSE THE PAST</span><span class="live-tag">SIMPLIFIED MODEL</span></div>
          <label class="range-label" for="cache-length">Prompt length <output id="cache-length-value">8 tokens</output></label>
          <input id="cache-length" type="range" min="4" max="24" step="4" value="8" />
          <div class="cache-legend"><span><i class="new-dot"></i> Recomputed / new</span><span><i class="saved-dot"></i> Reused from cache</span></div>
          <div class="cache-compare"><div><h4>Without cache</h4><div class="cache-grid" data-cache-uncached aria-hidden="true"></div><p><strong data-cache-uncached-count>30</strong> token positions projected</p></div><div><h4>With cache</h4><div class="cache-grid" data-cache-cached aria-hidden="true"></div><p><strong>3</strong> new token positions projected</p></div></div>
          <p class="demo-explanation" data-cache-explanation aria-live="polite"></p>
          <p class="book-note">Three decode steps after prefill. Each square represents a token position’s K/V projection. This comparison excludes prefill and does not estimate total FLOPs or speedup.</p>
          <span class="page-number">05</span>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="book-spread" id="spread-roofline" data-spread="roofline" aria-labelledby="title-roofline">
        <div class="book-page lesson-page">
          <p class="book-kicker">03 / THE ROOFLINE MODEL</p>
          <h3 id="title-roofline">Is the GPU waiting<br/><em>for the next byte?</em></h3>
          <p>A GPU can only calculate as fast as it can get its data. Two ceilings matter: <strong>memory bandwidth</strong> and <strong>compute throughput</strong>.</p>
          <p><strong>Arithmetic intensity</strong> means FLOPs performed per byte transferred. More reuse means more computation for each byte moved.</p>
          <div class="formula-card">Attainable TFLOP/s = min(<br/><span>peak TFLOP/s, FLOPs/byte × GB/s ÷ 1000</span>)</div>
          <p>Move the slider. Before the knee, bandwidth limits the ceiling. After it, compute does. Real kernels usually operate below this ideal roof.</p>
          <a class="chapter-link" href={`${base}/gpu-fundamentals/`}>Go deeper: GPU performance fundamentals →</a>
          <span class="page-number">12</span>
        </div>
        <div class="book-page experiment">
          <div class="experiment-heading"><span>TRY IT / FIND THE LIMIT</span><span class="live-tag">ILLUSTRATIVE GPU</span></div>
          <div class="hardware-spec"><span><b>100</b> TFLOP/s peak</span><span><b>1,000</b> GB/s bandwidth</span></div>
          <svg class="roofline-chart" viewBox="0 0 450 260" role="img" aria-labelledby="roof-chart-title roof-chart-desc"><title id="roof-chart-title">Interactive roofline ceiling</title><desc id="roof-chart-desc">Linear axes. The bandwidth ceiling rises until 100 FLOPs per byte, where it meets the 100 TFLOP per second compute ceiling. The marker shows the selected ideal limit.</desc><g class="chart-grid"><path d="M55 50H425M55 125H425M55 200H425"/></g><path d="M55 25V205H425" class="chart-axis"/><path d="M55 200L203 50H425" class="roof-path"/><path d="M55 200L203 50" class="memory-path"/><text x="8" y="54">100</text><text x="15" y="129">50</text><text x="22" y="204">0</text><text x="48" y="224">0</text><text x="190" y="224">100</text><text x="337" y="224">200</text><text x="172" y="250">Arithmetic intensity (FLOPs/byte)</text><text x="57" y="17">Ideal ceiling (TFLOP/s)</text><circle data-roof-point cx="85" cy="170" r="7"/><path data-roof-guide d="M85 200V170H55" class="point-guide"/></svg>
          <label class="range-label" for="roof-intensity">Arithmetic intensity <output id="roof-intensity-value">20 FLOPs/byte</output></label>
          <input id="roof-intensity" type="range" min="5" max="250" step="5" value="20" />
          <div class="roof-result"><strong data-roof-bound>Memory-bound</strong><span><b data-roof-ceiling>20</b> TFLOP/s ideal limit</span></div>
          <p class="demo-explanation" data-roof-explanation aria-live="polite"></p>
          <span class="page-number">13</span>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="book-spread" id="spread-precision" data-spread="precision" aria-labelledby="title-precision">
        <div class="book-page lesson-page">
          <p class="book-kicker">04 / WEIGHT PRECISION</p>
          <h3 id="title-precision">Smaller numbers.<br/><em>A lighter backpack.</em></h3>
          <p>A seven-billion-parameter model has seven billion stored weights. How many bits you use for each weight changes how much memory they occupy.</p>
          <div class="formula-card">Weight memory = parameters × bits ÷ 8</div>
          <p>Lower precision can make a model fit and reduce weight traffic. It can also change quality. Actual speed depends on supported kernels and the workload.</p>
          <div class="analogy"><span>CAPACITY IS NOT THE WHOLE STORY</span><p>You still need room for the KV cache, activations, workspace, and quantization metadata.</p></div>
          <a class="chapter-link" href={`${base}/quantization/`}>Go deeper: quantization →</a>
          <span class="page-number">08</span>
        </div>
        <div class="book-page experiment">
          <div class="experiment-heading"><span>TRY IT / PACK THE WEIGHTS</span><span class="live-tag">7B PARAMETER MODEL</span></div>
          <fieldset class="precision-options"><legend>Choose a weight representation</legend>{[{bits:32,label:'FP32'},{bits:16,label:'FP16'},{bits:8,label:'INT8'},{bits:4,label:'INT4'}].map(p => <label><input type="radio" name="weight-precision" value={p.bits} checked={p.bits===16}/><span>{p.label}<small>{p.bits} bits</small></span></label>)}</fieldset>
          <div class="memory-tank"><div class="memory-fill" data-weight-fill></div><div><strong data-weight-gb>14</strong><span>GB / weights only</span></div></div>
          <div class="memory-scale"><span>0 GB</span><span>28 GB (FP32 baseline)</span></div>
          <p class="demo-explanation" data-weight-explanation aria-live="polite">FP16 uses 14 GB for the raw weights of a 7B model.</p>
          <p class="book-note">Decimal GB (10⁹ bytes). Raw storage calculation, not a measured footprint or a promise that the model fits in a particular GPU.</p>
          <span class="page-number">09</span>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="book-spread" id="spread-batching" data-spread="batching" aria-labelledby="title-batching">
        <div class="book-page lesson-page">
          <p class="book-kicker">05 / CONTINUOUS BATCHING</p>
          <h3 id="title-batching">An empty seat?<br/><em>Let the next request in.</em></h3>
          <p>Requests finish at different times. With a fixed batch, a short request leaves an empty slot while its longer neighbors keep running.</p>
          <p><strong>Continuous batching</strong> admits a waiting request at a step boundary as soon as a slot becomes free.</p>
          <div class="analogy"><span>WHAT THE ANIMATION SHOWS</span><p>Six requests. Three slots. The same amount of token work, with fewer empty slots between requests.</p></div>
          <p>Real serving systems also balance prefill, KV memory, fairness, and latency. This toy schedule isolates decode-slot reuse.</p>
          <a class="chapter-link" href={`${base}/continuous-batching/`}>Go deeper: serving systems architecture →</a>
          <span class="page-number">10</span>
        </div>
        <div class="book-page experiment">
          <div class="experiment-heading"><span>TRY IT / WATCH THE SCHEDULE</span><span class="live-tag">UNIT DECODE STEPS</span></div>
          <div class="batch-requests" aria-label="Requests and required decode steps">{[2,5,3,2,4,1].map((n,i)=><span class={`request-${i}`}>{String.fromCharCode(65+i)} <small>{n} {n === 1 ? 'step' : 'steps'}</small></span>)}</div>
          <div class="schedule-compare"><div><h4>Fixed batches <span data-fixed-count></span></h4><div class="schedule" data-fixed-schedule></div></div><div><h4>Continuous <span data-continuous-count></span></h4><div class="schedule" data-continuous-schedule></div></div></div>
          <label class="range-label" for="batch-step">Decode step <output id="batch-step-value">0 / 9</output></label>
          <input id="batch-step" type="range" min="0" max="9" step="1" value="0"/>
          <div class="demo-controls"><button class="book-button" data-batch-play>Play schedule →</button><button class="quiet-button" data-batch-reset>Reset</button></div>
          <p class="demo-explanation" data-batch-explanation aria-live="polite"></p>
          <p class="book-note">All requests arrive at time zero; each generated token costs one equal step; prefill and scheduling overhead are excluded. Colors identify requests, not measured GPU utilization.</p>
          <span class="page-number">11</span>
        </div>
      </section>
